<p>Italian police said they arrested Roberto Mazzarella, whom authorities describe as one of Italy’s most wanted fugitives, during a raid on a luxury villa on the Amalfi Coast on Friday. Officials said Mazzarella is part of the Naples-based Camorra and is wanted on murder charges linked to a 2000 fatal shooting at a delicatessen in central Naples. Prime Minister Giorgia Meloni called the arrest an important blow against the Camorra and said it showed the Italian state would not back down.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/3E5okvGlAj6fH2iZw_AY_9Xu4E6GciwPoF3Ln2zWdbP0WWVtY5c5k4yb7hpWoVvmJmg1pQMg87-zzbR4PTW-lk69p_dQpeQF8HYENZ0nj-mW8lo8mDeQqI2ay583pOjEJVy5Ey_O9Ap8_EqYB4illylvjdxfHFJrW1Hq2liiKg' alt='Image accompanying ABC News coverage of the arrest of Roberto Mazzarella at an Italian resort.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>False documents: Authorities said Mazzarella allegedly used false documents to rent the coastal villa where police found him.</li><li>Age identified: Italian authorities said Mazzarella is 48 years old when they announced the arrest.</li><li>Lead agencies: Meloni specifically praised Naples Carabinieri investigators and the district anti-mafia directorate for carrying out the capture.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Giorgia Meloni: The prime minister described the arrest as an important blow against the Camorra and a sign that the state remains determined in its anti-mafia campaign. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/meloni-hails-arrest-top-crime-family-suspect-after-131732494'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>Italian police: Police described the operation as the capture of one of Italy’s most dangerous fugitives after more than a year in hiding. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/04/fugitive-mafia-boss-wanted-arrested-roberto-mazzarella'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/04/fugitive-mafia-boss-wanted-arrested-roberto-mazzarella'>Fugitive 